question:
5. (1990 AIME Problem 8) \( n \) is the smallest positive integer that satisfies the following conditions:
(1) \( n \) is a multiple of 75;
(2) \( n \) has exactly 75 positive divisors (including 1 and itself). Find \( \frac{n}{75} \).

Define a new function \( g \) on positive integers as follows:
\[ g(n) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
n + 15 & \text{if \( n < 15 \)}, \\
g(n - 7) & \text{if \( n \ge 15 \)}.
\end{array}
\right. \]
Find the maximum value of the function \( g \).

Given an increasing geometric sequence $\{a_n\}$ such that $a_2+a_3+a_4=28$, and $a_3+2$ is the arithmetic mean of $a_2$ and $a_4$,  
(I) Find the general formula for the sequence $\{a_n\}$;  
(II) Let $b_n = a_n \log_{\frac{1}{2}} a_n$ and $S_n = b_1 + b_2 + \ldots + b_n$, find the smallest positive integer $n$ such that $S_n + n \cdot 2^{n+1} > 62$.

Given all triangles have the same value, and all circles have the same value, find the sum of three circles:
\begin{align*}
\Delta + \bigcirc + \Delta + \bigcirc + 3\Delta &= 27\\
3\bigcirc + 2\Delta + \bigcirc + 2\Delta + \bigcirc &= 29\\
3\bigcirc &= \ ?
\end{align*}

Find the number of ordered quintuples $(a,b,c,d,e)$ of nonnegative real numbers such that
\begin{align*}
a^2 + b^2 + c^3 + d^3 + e^3 &= 5, \\
(a + b + c + d + e)(a^3 + b^3 + c^2 + d^2 + e^2) &= 25.
\end{align*}

Evaluate the following:

(1) Simplify and then evaluate: $(x-5y)(-x-5y)-( -x+5y)^2$, where $x=0.5$, $y=-1$;

(2) Given $a^2 -2a + b^2 + 4b + 5 = 0$, find the value of $(a+b)^{2013}$.

The eccentricity of the ellipse $C$: $\dfrac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\dfrac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1(a > b > 0)$ is $\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$, and one of its vertices is on the directrix of the parabola $x^{2}=4y$.

(Ⅰ) Find the equation of the ellipse $C$;

(Ⅱ) Let $O$ be the origin, and $M, N$ be two different points on the ellipse, with the slopes of lines $OM$ and $ON$ being $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$, respectively. When $k_{1}k_{2}=-\dfrac{1}{4}$, discuss whether the area of $\triangle MON$ is constant. If it is constant, find the value; if not, explain why.
